## Introduction

When screed separates from a wall, the bond failed — and bonding agents are the chemistry that makes the difference. But the market is crowded with products making the same promise, and the wrong choice (or the right choice, misused) gives a false sense of security that fails at the worst possible moment.

This guide explains how bonding agents actually work, the types available in Nigerian building material markets, which surfaces genuinely need them and which just need proper preparation, correct application rates and open times, and where a simple cement slurry key does the same job for a fraction of the price.

## How Bond Actually Works — the Two Mechanisms

Screed holds to a wall through two different mechanisms, and every bonding decision is about which one the surface allows:

- **Mechanical key:** the coat grips the surface's tooth — open blockwork pores, hacked render, scratched plaster. The screed shrinks slightly as it cures and *wrings itself onto* the tooth. This is the primary bond on normal masonry, and no bottle can substitute for it.
- **Chemical adhesion:** the glue line — polymer-modified surfaces, bonding slurries, and SBR/PVA families — bridges where mechanical tooth is unavailable (smooth concrete, sealed faces) and adds a margin where it is partial.

The professional rule: **key first, chemistry second.** Bonding agents are a bridge to surfaces that cannot be keyed, and a reinforcement where keying is marginal — never a substitute for preparation. The product that promises "just paint it on over dust" is selling the dust a commission.

## What the Nigerian Market Actually Sells

Walk any building materials market and the "bonding" shelf is three families, whatever the brand names say:

**SBR (styrene-butadiene rubber) latex liquids** — the market's workhorse. White/milky liquid, gauged into slurries or the mix, flexible film former. It is a *gauging* material (part of the water, never an additive on top of it), and its slurry — SBR + cement + water to a paintable cream, sometimes splashed to a spatter — is the classic bridge for smooth surfaces and patch repairs. Genuine and effective; also the most counterfeited chemistry on the shelf, which makes the supplier matter (below).

**PVA (polyvinyl acetate) families** — the white glues and "gum" products. Cheaper, everywhere, and honest about their limits: PVA re-emulsifies with water, so it belongs **indoors only**, in dry internal work. The classic failure — PVA used in a wet area or on an exterior wall, re-softened by the first season's damp, and the coat that "bonded perfectly" sheeting off — is a market-wide recurring story, not a bad batch.

**Cementitious/proprietary bonding primers** — pre-bagged brush-applied primers and key-coats from the admixture companies. Consistent, mix-to-print instructions, and priced accordingly. For a large, smooth-surface campaign the consistency is worth the premium; for a single wall, the SBR slurry does the same chemistry for a fraction.

And the fourth "product" that is not on the shelf: **plain cement slurry** — cement and water (often with a sand fleck) brushed or splashed ahead of the coat. It is the oldest bonding bridge in the trade, it is what the expensive products mostly deliver in a fancier bottle, and on a porous, high-suction surface it is often *all* the wall ever needed.

## Which Surfaces Need What — the Decision Table

| Surface | Needs | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Fresh, open-textured blockwork | Preparation only — wash, dampen, no agent | Full mechanical key available; dampening controls suction |
| Keyed/rendered masonry | Preparation only | Same — tooth present |
| Smooth float-finished render | Hack for key, or SBR slurry | Tooth absent; bridge or make tooth |
| Smooth structural concrete | SBR slurry or proprietary primer (hacking often impractical) | No tooth, low suction — the bridge's genuine case |
| Old paint (sound, well-adhered) | Best: strip. Else: hack + SBR slurry | Chemistry alone over a doubtful layer is a bet on the paint's bond, not yours |
| Gypsum-based old finishes | Do not bridge with cement — cut back | Chemistry conflict, not a bonding problem ([skimming vs plastering](/learn/skimming-vs-plastering-understanding-options)) |
| Damp walls / wet areas | SBR gauged systems; PVA never | PVA re-emulsifies; the wet area decides the family before the brand |

Two patterns read straight off the table: the agent earns its price exactly where tooth is missing, and **most "bonding failures" in Nigeria are actually preparation failures on surfaces that never needed a bottle** — the dust coat, the un-hacked gloss, the unwashed blockwork (see [wall preparation](/learn/how-to-prepare-walls-for-screeding)).

## Using Them Correctly — Rates, Open Time, and the Wet-On-Tacky Rule

The agents fail in use more often than in chemistry, on three rules:

1. **Gauge, don't pour.** SBR replaces part of the mix water per the maker's ratio — typically a few parts water to one part SBR for slurry work, per the product's own data. The "glug for luck" school of gauging is how films form where paste should be, and how the same product gets reviews ranging from "transformed my wall" to "ruined it."
2. **Open time is real.** The brushed slurry has a working window — the coat must follow while the bridge is *tacky*. A slurry allowed to cure becomes exactly what it was meant to prevent: a smooth, sealed layer between wall and coat. On a hot face the window is minutes; work in bays sized to your speed, or splash (spatter) instead of brush, so the tooth survives even a partial set.
3. **The surface conditions still apply.** Bonding agents tolerate a damper face than plain screed does (their genuine advantage in hot conditions), but no chemistry bonds to dust, oil, or a drummy layer. The assessment ([the four verdicts](/learn/complete-guide-wall-screeding-professional)) comes before the bottle, every time.

## Where the Cheap Answer Is the Right Answer

For calibration, the honest economics: an SBR slurry bridge on a 100 m² smooth-wall campaign costs real money (a drum of genuine SBR is one of the more expensive lines on the materials list); the cement-splash key on the same wall costs a couple of bags and an afternoon. The decision table above says which walls are which — and the industry's persistent failure is not overspending on agents, it is *bridging* walls that wanted washing, and *washing* walls that wanted a bridge.

The other cheap answer is the **spatter-dash**: SBR slurry flicked to a rough spatter coat and allowed to set — giving a smooth wall a manufactured tooth. It is the professional route on big concrete faces precisely because it converts a chemical problem (no key) into a mechanical one (a million tiny keys), and it lets the main coat run as an ordinary keyed application.

## Spotting the Fakes — a Market Reality

SBR is the most-faked product on the bonding shelf: diluted latex, relabelled PVA, and plain water with a story. Three honest defences: buy from the established admixture suppliers' dealers (the counterfeits cluster where prices are "special"); check consistency and smell against a known-genuine sample (SBR is milky, mildly ammoniacal, and consistent — not watery, not glue-sweet); and price-check against the market norm — a genuine SBR has a price floor, and a "promo" far under it is a chemistry lesson scheduled for your wall. One more: date of manufacture matters for latex products — old stock separates; shake-test before buying.


## Bonding for Patch Repairs — Where Agents Earn Their Reputation

The bonding agents' most convincing work is not whole-wall campaigns but *patching*: the cut-out repair where new screed meets old coat at four edges. A patch is the hardest bond in the trade — small, edge-loaded, and shrinkage-concentrated — and the professional patch recipe is a checklist of this guide's rules: cut back to sound edges with square (not feathered) borders; dampen the host; SBR-gauged slurry brushed tacky-wet across the host and edges; the patch mix gauged with SBR in the water as well; and the cure that a small patch never gets unless someone insists (misted, shaded, a few days). Done this way, the patch becomes indistinguishable from the wall; done with plain mix on a dusty cut, the patch is a second repair's site, scheduled for the next season.

The same logic scales up to the *bandage* repair — mesh-bridged SBR renders over movement cracks — where the flexible chemistry accommodates what the rigid coat could not, and the crack's signature stops re-appearing through the paint each year. Both jobs are why experienced screeders keep a small SBR supply on the truck at all times: it is the trade's first-aid kit.

## Storage and Shelf Life — the Unpriced Risk

The agent's chemistry has a clock: latex liquids separate and sour in heat, frozen drums rupture, and a part-used container left open loses its water to the film that forms on top. The honest habits: buy for the job, not for the decade; store sealed, shaded, and off hot floors; stir thoroughly before use (separation is recoverable, skinning and souring are not); and date-mark opened containers — an SBR that has sat through two harmattans before being "used up" is a bond failure looking for a wall. On the cost side this is the quiet reason agents disappoint: the failed coats are traced to the product, and the post-mortem finds the product was two years past its best in an open drum behind the store.

## Closing

Bond is tooth first, chemistry second: key what can be keyed, bridge what cannot with the right family (SBR outdoors and wet, PVA indoors and dry, plain slurry where suction was the only problem), apply per the maker's gauge and the tacky-window rule — and remember that most failed bonds never needed a product at all, only a washed wall and a counted batch. For the surfaces that lead into each route, see [wall preparation](/learn/how-to-prepare-walls-for-screeding); for what the whole campaign consumes, [the quantities guide](/learn/how-to-calculate-screeding-material-quantities) and [the costs guide](/learn/how-to-estimate-screeding-costs-nigeria). The wall that gets a bottle before a verdict was the wall that needed a bucket of wash water all along — buy the chemistry last, and buy only what the surface's own tests asked for.
